<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  The air-launched rails: a LAU-7 with an AIM-9J, a LAU-128 with an AIM-120C-7 and a LAU-118 with an
  AGM-88 HARM. Nothing on any of them moves, so each is laid from the moment it exists and is aimed by
  pointing the craft. Part definitions are in ../KSArmory*.xml, which KSA reads and this does not.
-->
<WeaponPack Schema="1">

  <!--
    AIM-9J. Airframe figures are the real weapon's: 3.05 m, 127 mm, 78 kg, about Mach 2.5 off its
    motor, some 14 km. It homes on the target itself, so it keeps guiding after the launcher loses
    the track, and can be thrown off by a target that leaves its field of view.

    The Mk 17 is a booster, not a sustainer: 2.2 s and then a coast the whole rest of the way, which is
    why its reach depends on launch speed and height and a long shot arrives slow. BoostAccel is the
    2.2 s and the Mach 2.5 peak divided, since a model with no mass can only take the average.

    A lead-sulphide cell with no counter-countermeasures: it follows the brightest thing in its field,
    which a flare is for its first seconds. SeparationSeconds is about 40 m off the rail at 25 m/s
    under 370 m/s^2, clear of the craft before anything turns it. No fin marker: a rail-launched round
    carries its fins deployed.
  -->
  <Munition Name="AIM9J" DisplayName="AIM-9J Sidewinder"
            BodyMarker="Aim9" BodyLength="3.05"
            MinRange="400" MaxRange="14000"
            LaunchSpeed="25" BoostSeconds="2.2" BoostAccel="370" MaxFlightSeconds="40"
            DragK="4.2e-5"
            Guidance="Seeker" SeekerFovDeg="40" SeekerBand="Infrared" CountermeasureResistance="0.1"
            NavConstant="4" MaxLateralG="30" SeparationSeconds="0.4"
            FuseRadius="12" FuseArmSeconds="1.0" ChargeKg="9.4" />

  <!--
    AIM-120C-7. Airframe figures are the real weapon's: 3.65 m, 178 mm, 161.5 kg, a 20 kg warhead.
    What separates it from the AIM-9J is reach: boost-only to about Mach 4 and a coast from there, so a
    shot at the edge of its envelope arrives slow and turns badly. MaxRange is from altitude and speed;
    a low shot gets nothing like it.

    Modelled as Seeker because that is what it does at the end. The real round flies a mid-course
    datalink, which this mod does not model, and a command-linked round would break the moment the
    launcher lost the track - the one thing an AMRAAM is built not to do. The wide gimbal is that
    compromise. Pulse-Doppler: chaff that stops dead is outside the gate unless the target beams.
  -->
  <Munition Name="AIM120C" DisplayName="AIM-120C-7 AMRAAM"
            BodyMarker="Amraam_Round" BodyLength="3.645"
            MinRange="900" MaxRange="105000"
            LaunchSpeed="25" BoostSeconds="5.5" BoostAccel="235" MaxFlightSeconds="120"
            DragK="2.6e-5"
            Guidance="Seeker" SeekerFovDeg="60" SeekerBand="Radar" CountermeasureResistance="0.3"
            DopplerGateMps="40"
            NavConstant="4" MaxLateralG="40" SeparationSeconds="0.4"
            FuseRadius="14" FuseArmSeconds="1.2" ChargeKg="20" />

  <!--
    AGM-88 HARM. Airframe figures are the real weapon's, from designation-systems.net: 4.17 m, 254 mm,
    360 kg, a 66 kg WDU-21/B warhead. It homes on an emission, so a contact that is not transmitting is
    not a target however large or close: useless against aircraft, decisive against a radar site.

    The SR113-TC-1 is dual-thrust: the boost is BoostSeconds/BoostAccel and the Stage is the
    sustainer, still pushing where a coasting round has started to fall back. The wide gimbal is
    direction-finding across the forward hemisphere rather than a dish that has to be pointed.
  -->
  <Munition Name="AGM88" DisplayName="AGM-88 HARM"
            BodyMarker="Harm_Round" BodyLength="4.17"
            MinRange="1200" MaxRange="148000"
            LaunchSpeed="25" BoostSeconds="5.0" BoostAccel="210" MaxFlightSeconds="180"
            DragK="3.1e-5"
            Guidance="AntiRadiation" SeekerFovDeg="70"
            NavConstant="4" MaxLateralG="20" SeparationSeconds="0.4"
            FuseRadius="16" FuseArmSeconds="1.5" ChargeKg="66">
    <Stage Seconds="18.0" Accel="26" />
  </Munition>

  <!--
    The rails' search sets are the rounds' seekers, standing in for whatever is carrying the rail,
    since nothing on a rail searches. PartForward because a seeker looks where the rail points; local
    up would have a rail on a booster searching whichever way gravity lay.
  -->
  <Sensor Name="AIM9SEEK" DisplayName="AIM-9 seeker head"
          Range="16000" ConeDeg="50" BoresightSource="PartForward"
          ThreatRadius="6000" ThreatHorizonSeconds="30" LockSeconds="1.0" />

  <!--
    Radar rather than infrared, so it does not need the target hot: a fighter-sized return, and
    detection goes as the fourth root of cross-section. Active, so the round itself is something an
    anti-radiation weapon could home on.
  -->
  <Sensor Name="AIM120SEEK" DisplayName="AIM-120 seeker head"
          Range="40000" ConeDeg="60" BoresightSource="PartForward"
          ThreatRadius="30000" ThreatHorizonSeconds="60" LockSeconds="1.4"
          ReferenceCrossSectionM2="5" Emits="true" />

  <!--
    Wholly passive, and that is the weapon: a craft carrying only this rail is invisible to another
    anti-radiation round. It hears a transmitter far beyond where its return would be seen, whatever
    its size, so there is no cross-section; and a radar site sits still, so no minimum speed.
  -->
  <Sensor Name="AGM88SEEK" DisplayName="AGM-88 seeker head"
          Range="60000" ConeDeg="70" BoresightSource="PartForward" Scope="Emitters"
          ThreatRadius="50000" ThreatHorizonSeconds="90" LockSeconds="1.0"
          MinTargetSpeed="0" Emits="false" />

  <!--
    One tube and no reload, so a rail has exactly one shot; a second rail is a second weapon with its
    own magazine. The tube is the nose of the seated round and the direction it leaves along, since a
    fixed launcher has no pods to give it an axis.

    Off the rail, then a turn: the round leaves along the rail pushed clear of the mount, coasts for its
    SeparationSeconds, and only then steers, bounded by its own lateral G. A rail on a stack cannot be
    aimed by pointing the craft, so a ground designation sits 92-116 degrees off it; that limits the
    seeker, not the weapon (Interceptor's SeekerInView, FireGate.CanGuideOntoAimpoint).

    LAU-7 geometry is printed by tools/model/sidewinder.py. The LAU-128's and LAU-118's art is authored,
    so their tube is the seat position in KSArmoryAssets.xml plus half the mesh's length; the .blend is
    not in this repository, and tools/validate-parts.py is the only thing holding the three together.
  -->
  <Launcher PartId="KSArmory_Prefab_SidewinderRail" DisplayName="LAU-7 Sidewinder rail"
            FireControlLabel="LAU-7 fire control"
            Munition="AIM9J" Sensor="AIM9SEEK" StoreMassKg="78"
            MuzzleForwardOffset="0.408"
            LaunchAlongTube="true" EjectAwayFromMount="0.55" LaunchLoft="0" MuzzleOffset="2"
            ReloadSeconds="0" SettleSeconds="0">
    <Tube Position="0.40850, 1.52500, 0.00000" Direction="0, 1, 0" />
  </Launcher>

  <Launcher PartId="KSArmory_Prefab_AmraamRail" DisplayName="LAU-128 AMRAAM rail"
            FireControlLabel="LAU-128 fire control"
            Munition="AIM120C" Sensor="AIM120SEEK" StoreMassKg="161.5"
            MuzzleForwardOffset="0.424"
            LaunchAlongTube="true" EjectAwayFromMount="0.55" LaunchLoft="0" MuzzleOffset="2"
            ReloadSeconds="0" SettleSeconds="0">
    <Tube Position="0.42400, 1.82250, 0.00000" Direction="0, 1, 0" />
  </Launcher>

  <Launcher PartId="KSArmory_Prefab_HarmRail" DisplayName="LAU-118 HARM rail"
            FireControlLabel="LAU-118 fire control"
            Munition="AGM88" Sensor="AGM88SEEK" StoreMassKg="360"
            MuzzleForwardOffset="0.540"
            LaunchAlongTube="true" EjectAwayFromMount="0.55" LaunchLoft="0" MuzzleOffset="2"
            ReloadSeconds="0" SettleSeconds="0">
    <Tube Position="0.54000, 2.08500, 0.00000" Direction="0, 1, 0" />
  </Launcher>

</WeaponPack>
